course: dictionary
lesson: 4300
title: Reading a grin
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A grin is a smile that spreads wide, often so wide you can see the teeth. «Dad grins: «That's my boy.»» Dad is pleased, and the grin shows it without any need for words.
explain: A grin can say more than the words do. «Then a grin appears in the air: the Cheshire Cat.» A grin on its own, with no face or body around it, is strange and a bit spooky, so the grin is the thing that the reader notices first.
explain: Grin is also a verb, so it can change: grin, grins, grinned, grinning. «A big cat grinning from ear to ear» means the cat is smiling very widely. Look at the words around grin to work out whether the grin is happy, cheeky or mischievous.
explain: Sometimes the phrase «grin and bear it» appears. It does not mean smile. It means to put up with something unpleasant without complaining. Context helps here: if someone is hurt or tired and still keeps quiet, they are grinning and bearing it.
fact: Grin can be a verb (to grin) or a noun (a grin).
fact: To grin means to smile widely, often showing your teeth.
fact: A grin can be happy, cheeky or mischievous, and the words around it tell you which.
fact: «Grin and bear it» means to put up with something unpleasant without complaining.
fact: Phrases you may meet: grin from ear to ear, a cheeky grin, a big grin, wipe that grin off your face.
exercise: «Dad grins: «That's my boy.»» What does grins mean here? | Shouts in anger | Runs out of the room | Falls asleep | *Smiles widely, showing his teeth
exercise: «...a big cat grinning from ear to ear.» What does «from ear to ear» tell you? | The cat is cold and shivering | *The smile is very wide | The cat is asleep | The cat has very big ears
exercise: She had a sore knee, but she decided to grin and bear it. What does grin and bear it mean? | Smile at everyone you meet | Carry a heavy bag with a smile | *Put up with something unpleasant without complaining | Laugh loudly at a joke
test: Tom gave a cheeky grin when he took the last biscuit. What does a cheeky grin show? | Anger | Sadness | Tiredness | *Mischief, a playful naughtiness
test: «The rule is tone: a grin and a mate's shoulder bump make a tease; an angry face makes an insult.» What is the difference? | A grin always makes people cry | Grins and angry faces mean the same thing | A tease is a kind of angry face | *A grin can make a joke friendly, but an angry face turns it into an insult
exam: Which word is a noun in this sentence: «She gave me a big grin»? | *grin | She | gave | me
